1
00:00:00,978 --> 00:00:02,009
On day 331,

2
00:00:02,009 --> 00:00:04,907
nobody talks about anything else.

3
00:00:04,907 --> 00:00:06,943
The sun comes up over the hill,

4
00:00:06,943 --> 00:00:09,127
and the long room is already full

5
00:00:09,127 --> 00:00:11,138
of people pretending they slept.

6
00:00:11,138 --> 00:00:13,430
Ruth sits by the window with the radio

7
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:15,321
in her lap, the way she sat all night.

8
00:00:15,679 --> 00:00:17,499
Kit is asleep against her shoulder,

9
00:00:17,499 --> 00:00:19,333
still wearing his white helmet.

10
00:00:19,333 --> 00:00:21,428
The radio says nothing at all.

11
00:00:21,428 --> 00:00:22,976
Your name is Patch.

12
00:00:22,976 --> 00:00:24,160
Three hundred and thirty days ago,

13
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:27,033
you were waiting for a bus.

14
00:00:27,033 --> 00:00:29,492
Since then, you have lived under a bus depot,

15
00:00:29,492 --> 00:00:30,769
and then above it,

16
00:00:30,769 --> 00:00:34,172
on a hill, looking down at a yard so full of

17
00:00:34,172 --> 00:00:35,893
the dead that you can't see the ground.

18
00:00:35,893 --> 00:00:37,189
You put them there yourself,

19
00:00:37,189 --> 00:00:39,146
two weeks ago, so nineteen people in the

20
00:00:39,146 --> 00:00:41,718
city could have their bridge back.

21
00:00:41,718 --> 00:00:43,592
And last night, a man called Tam

22
00:00:43,592 --> 00:00:45,391
spoke out of the radio from a

23
00:00:45,391 --> 00:00:47,260
rock in the sea, and said the gate

24
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:50,024
at the end of his road is leaning.

25
00:00:50,024 --> 00:00:52,170
"Five weeks," says Ruth.

26
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:54,068
"We walked here in nineteen days.

27
00:00:54,068 --> 00:00:55,633
We can walk back."

28
00:00:56,201 --> 00:00:58,291
"And arrive with two weeks to

29
00:00:58,291 --> 00:01:01,405
spare," says Wes, "and three pairs of feet,

30
00:01:01,405 --> 00:01:03,374
and no way to carry anything."

31
00:01:03,990 --> 00:01:04,745
"Then what?"

32
00:01:04,745 --> 00:01:07,683
Vera is standing at the big window,

33
00:01:07,683 --> 00:01:09,468
looking down at the depot.

34
00:01:09,468 --> 00:01:12,321
At the yard, the canopy, the sign.

35
00:01:12,321 --> 00:01:14,082
At the dark rectangle in the

36
00:01:14,082 --> 00:01:15,789
middle of all those heads,

37
00:01:15,789 --> 00:01:19,049
where something big and beige used to stand.

38
00:01:19,568 --> 00:01:21,312
She takes the travel alarm clock out

39
00:01:21,312 --> 00:01:23,403
of her pocket and listens to it tick.

40
00:01:23,826 --> 00:01:25,766
"Then we drive," she says.

41
00:01:25,766 --> 00:01:26,842
"In a bus."

42
00:01:27,441 --> 00:01:28,864
Everybody looks at you.

43
00:01:28,864 --> 00:01:31,073
They always do, at this point.

44
00:01:31,073 --> 00:01:32,404
And you realize that the

45
00:01:32,404 --> 00:01:34,940
question isn't really about buses at all.

46
00:01:35,347 --> 00:01:38,754
It's the question Ruth asked on day 285,

47
00:01:38,754 --> 00:01:39,600
by the fire,

48
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:43,061
when you said "When the roads are clear," and Nora

49
00:01:43,061 --> 00:01:45,007
smiled at the first half of a yes.

50
00:01:45,007 --> 00:01:47,076
You look down at the yard.

51
00:01:47,076 --> 00:01:49,558
There's no bunker to go back to now.

52
00:01:49,558 --> 00:01:50,582
No bench.

53
00:01:50,582 --> 00:01:51,626
No lantern.

54
00:01:51,626 --> 00:01:53,888
Only a sign over a street,

55
00:01:53,888 --> 00:01:56,235
and a gate with the dead behind it.

56
00:01:56,235 --> 00:01:57,454
"Then we'd better go

57
00:01:57,454 --> 00:01:59,955
and get it," you say.

58
00:01:59,955 --> 00:02:02,491
From day 332 to day 334,

59
00:02:02,491 --> 00:02:05,339
you go back to the pit.

60
00:02:05,713 --> 00:02:06,633
Four of you.

61
00:02:06,633 --> 00:02:09,095
You, Vera, Wes and Bram,

62
00:02:09,095 --> 00:02:11,486
in the white truck, the long way round,

63
00:02:11,486 --> 00:02:13,166
along the farm tracks,

64
00:02:13,166 --> 00:02:16,111
between fields that have finally started to turn green.

65
00:02:16,692 --> 00:02:18,740
You hear it before you see it.

66
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:19,334
Nothing.

67
00:02:19,334 --> 00:02:21,085
Just wind going around

68
00:02:21,085 --> 00:02:22,401
inside a hole in the ground.

69
00:02:22,401 --> 00:02:24,334
The gravel pit looks different now.

70
00:02:24,643 --> 00:02:26,399
One whole side of it is gone,

71
00:02:26,399 --> 00:02:28,795
slumped into a long brown slope,

72
00:02:28,795 --> 00:02:32,146
cracked and dry in the sun, from the bottom to the rim.

73
00:02:32,146 --> 00:02:35,411
The other side is the way you left it.

74
00:02:35,411 --> 00:02:35,931
Steep.

75
00:02:35,931 --> 00:02:36,641
Grey.

76
00:02:37,131 --> 00:02:38,093
The single road

77
00:02:38,093 --> 00:02:39,837
curving down along the wall.

78
00:02:39,837 --> 00:02:42,202
And across the top of the road,

79
00:02:42,202 --> 00:02:44,448
four sections of motorway barrier,

80
00:02:44,448 --> 00:02:48,365
still bolted end to end, still holding a gate across a

81
00:02:48,365 --> 00:02:51,283
place that hasn't needed one since the rain.

82
00:02:51,283 --> 00:02:52,257
At the bottom,

83
00:02:52,257 --> 00:02:54,525
where the frozen puddle used to be,

84
00:02:54,525 --> 00:02:57,199
there is a wide crust of dried mud.

85
00:02:57,199 --> 00:02:58,607
And in the middle of it,

86
00:02:58,607 --> 00:03:00,913
still standing on its wheels,

87
00:03:00,913 --> 00:03:03,660
mud up to its hubcaps, is the coach.

88
00:03:04,035 --> 00:03:04,882
Around it,

89
00:03:04,882 --> 00:03:08,964
like fence posts, stand the last of the dead.

90
00:03:08,964 --> 00:03:10,229
Forty, maybe.

91
00:03:10,229 --> 00:03:12,499
Sunk to the knee in the mud,

92
00:03:12,499 --> 00:03:13,503
facing the bus,

93
00:03:13,503 --> 00:03:16,639
the way they faced it on the night the horn died.

94
00:03:16,639 --> 00:03:18,486
They haven't moved in weeks.

95
00:03:18,486 --> 00:03:21,005
They don't look like they ever plan to.

96
00:03:21,005 --> 00:03:23,071
"They never follow anything

97
00:03:23,071 --> 00:03:25,225
the first time," says Vera softly.

98
00:03:25,873 --> 00:03:26,926
Wes looks at her.

99
00:03:26,926 --> 00:03:28,037
"Then we'll have to

100
00:03:28,037 --> 00:03:30,190
give them a second time."

101
00:03:30,190 --> 00:03:31,272
On day 335,

102
00:03:31,272 --> 00:03:33,816
the clock does one more job.

103
00:03:33,816 --> 00:03:35,250
The plan is Vera's.

104
00:03:35,250 --> 00:03:37,257
She winds the travel alarm

105
00:03:37,257 --> 00:03:38,747
clock all the way,

106
00:03:38,747 --> 00:03:42,125
sets it, and ties it into the end of the rope.

107
00:03:42,125 --> 00:03:43,592
Then she walks around the rim,

108
00:03:43,592 --> 00:03:45,175
to the far side of the pit,

109
00:03:45,175 --> 00:03:47,587
the steep side, opposite the coach.

110
00:03:48,169 --> 00:03:50,529
You lower the clock down the wall,

111
00:03:50,529 --> 00:03:52,200
slowly, hand over hand,

112
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:55,442
until it hangs just above the mud at the bottom.

113
00:03:56,024 --> 00:03:57,050
And it rings.

114
00:03:57,050 --> 00:03:59,069
A thin little sound,

115
00:03:59,069 --> 00:04:02,850
tinny and brave, rattling around the bottom of a gravel pit.

116
00:04:03,384 --> 00:04:04,683
Down by the coach,

117
00:04:04,683 --> 00:04:06,555
forty heads lift at once.

118
00:04:06,945 --> 00:04:08,794
Slowly, very slowly,

119
00:04:08,794 --> 00:04:12,283
the first one pulls one foot out of the mud.

120
00:04:12,283 --> 00:04:13,258
Then the other.

121
00:04:13,258 --> 00:04:15,335
And then they are walking,

122
00:04:15,335 --> 00:04:16,070
all of them,

123
00:04:16,070 --> 00:04:19,603
sucking and squelching across the floor of the pit,

124
00:04:19,603 --> 00:04:21,967
away from the coach, toward the sound.

125
00:04:21,967 --> 00:04:23,057
It takes them the

126
00:04:23,057 --> 00:04:24,987
whole afternoon to cross.

127
00:04:24,987 --> 00:04:26,624
When the bell runs down,

128
00:04:26,624 --> 00:04:29,680
they're standing at the bottom of the far wall,

129
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:31,301
in a little crowd,

130
00:04:31,301 --> 00:04:33,582
looking up at a clock they can't reach.

131
00:04:33,582 --> 00:04:34,908
They won't climb.

132
00:04:35,331 --> 00:04:36,271
You know that.

133
00:04:36,271 --> 00:04:37,587
And there is nothing

134
00:04:37,587 --> 00:04:39,195
on that side to walk up.

135
00:04:39,195 --> 00:04:40,961
Vera ties off the rope and

136
00:04:40,961 --> 00:04:43,791
leaves the clock hanging there.

137
00:04:43,791 --> 00:04:45,472
"Twice," she says.

138
00:04:45,991 --> 00:04:48,071
"It's done its job twice now.

139
00:04:48,071 --> 00:04:51,093
Most people don't get that."

140
00:04:51,093 --> 00:04:53,376
On day 336,

141
00:04:53,376 --> 00:04:54,824
you unbolt the gate.

142
00:04:54,824 --> 00:04:55,789
Bram and you,

143
00:04:55,789 --> 00:04:58,984
with Ansel's spanners, at the top of the road.

144
00:04:58,984 --> 00:05:01,351
The bolts squeal like gulls again.

145
00:05:01,806 --> 00:05:02,493
One by one,

146
00:05:02,493 --> 00:05:04,678
the four sections of barrier come up,

147
00:05:04,678 --> 00:05:07,222
and the road down into the pit is open.

148
00:05:07,222 --> 00:05:08,363
Bram looks at the

149
00:05:08,363 --> 00:05:10,324
barrier lying in the grass.

150
00:05:10,324 --> 00:05:11,993
Then he picks up the first section

151
00:05:11,993 --> 00:05:14,260
and puts it on the back of the truck.

152
00:05:14,260 --> 00:05:15,396
Then the second.

153
00:05:15,396 --> 00:05:16,767
"We're not going to

154
00:05:16,767 --> 00:05:18,713
need a gate," says Wes.

155
00:05:18,713 --> 00:05:21,244
Bram picks up the third section,

156
00:05:21,244 --> 00:05:23,287
and looks at him, and whistles two notes.

157
00:05:23,287 --> 00:05:24,765
Then a third.

158
00:05:24,765 --> 00:05:26,772
"Maybe," Wes translates.

159
00:05:26,772 --> 00:05:27,681
"Fine.

160
00:05:27,681 --> 00:05:28,722
Maybe."

161
00:05:28,722 --> 00:05:30,359
On day 337,

162
00:05:30,359 --> 00:05:32,915
the coach wakes up a second time.

163
00:05:32,915 --> 00:05:35,462
You drive the truck down the long curving road,

164
00:05:35,462 --> 00:05:37,094
slowly, into the pit,

165
00:05:37,094 --> 00:05:40,602
with the forty dead watching you from the far wall.

166
00:05:40,602 --> 00:05:42,020
Nobody breathes much.

167
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:44,324
They watch you the whole way down,

168
00:05:44,324 --> 00:05:45,282
but they don't come.

169
00:05:45,282 --> 00:05:47,394
They are waiting for their clock.

170
00:05:47,394 --> 00:05:49,457
You park nose to nose with the coach,

171
00:05:49,457 --> 00:05:51,398
the way you did on the motorway.

172
00:05:51,398 --> 00:05:52,866
Bram opens the coach's

173
00:05:52,866 --> 00:05:55,205
engine flap and stares into it for

174
00:05:55,205 --> 00:05:56,975
a long time, without a word.

175
00:05:56,975 --> 00:05:58,430
There is dried mud in

176
00:05:58,430 --> 00:06:00,201
places mud should never be.

177
00:06:00,201 --> 00:06:02,058
He takes things out.

178
00:06:02,058 --> 00:06:02,926
He wipes them.

179
00:06:02,926 --> 00:06:04,025
He puts them back.

180
00:06:04,025 --> 00:06:05,771
It takes him until noon.

181
00:06:06,304 --> 00:06:08,531
Then Wes clips on the jump leads.

182
00:06:08,531 --> 00:06:09,910
Red to red.

183
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:11,009
Black to black.

184
00:06:11,009 --> 00:06:13,371
Three times, with hand movements.

185
00:06:13,986 --> 00:06:15,995
Vera climbs into the driver's seat.

186
00:06:15,995 --> 00:06:18,669
She puts both hands on the big wheel,

187
00:06:18,669 --> 00:06:19,878
and turns the key.

188
00:06:19,878 --> 00:06:21,088
The starter turns.

189
00:06:21,088 --> 00:06:21,897
Slowly.

190
00:06:22,272 --> 00:06:22,925
Once.

191
00:06:22,925 --> 00:06:23,687
Twice.

192
00:06:23,687 --> 00:06:25,510
Then nothing.

193
00:06:26,125 --> 00:06:27,217
At the far wall,

194
00:06:27,217 --> 00:06:29,572
forty heads turn toward the sound.

195
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:31,298
"Again," says Bram.

196
00:06:31,298 --> 00:06:32,794
It is the first word you

197
00:06:32,794 --> 00:06:34,811
have ever heard him say.

198
00:06:34,811 --> 00:06:36,424
Vera turns the key again.

199
00:06:36,424 --> 00:06:37,841
The coach coughs.

200
00:06:38,168 --> 00:06:38,788
Coughs.

201
00:06:38,788 --> 00:06:39,873
Coughs again,

202
00:06:39,873 --> 00:06:43,593
a great rattling cough, the kind of cough that makes

203
00:06:43,593 --> 00:06:45,955
everybody in a waiting room look up.

204
00:06:45,955 --> 00:06:47,354
And then it catches.

205
00:06:47,354 --> 00:06:49,686
A deep diesel rumble fills the pit,

206
00:06:49,686 --> 00:06:52,699
and the forty start across the mud toward you,

207
00:06:52,699 --> 00:06:55,788
slowly, one foot after the other.

208
00:06:55,788 --> 00:06:57,418
"Go," says Wes.

209
00:06:57,418 --> 00:07:00,149
The wheels spin in the mud.

210
00:07:00,149 --> 00:07:00,671
Spin.

211
00:07:00,671 --> 00:07:01,629
Then grip.

212
00:07:01,629 --> 00:07:04,892
The coach climbs out of its own tracks,

213
00:07:04,892 --> 00:07:06,517
the truck reversing ahead of it,

214
00:07:06,517 --> 00:07:09,585
up the long curving road, past the place where it

215
00:07:09,585 --> 00:07:12,767
slid on the ice, all the way to the top.

216
00:07:12,767 --> 00:07:15,107
Behind you, the forty reach the bottom

217
00:07:15,107 --> 00:07:16,829
of the road, and stop,

218
00:07:16,829 --> 00:07:20,197
because the engines are already too far away to follow.

219
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:23,160
At the rim, Vera switches off and

220
00:07:23,160 --> 00:07:26,354
sits there with her hands on the wheel.

221
00:07:26,354 --> 00:07:28,289
"Good bus," she says.

222
00:07:28,807 --> 00:07:33,941
From day 338 to day 342,

223
00:07:33,941 --> 00:07:35,723
the city fills the tank.

224
00:07:35,723 --> 00:07:39,389
You drive the coach slowly along the farm tracks,

225
00:07:39,389 --> 00:07:40,630
with the truck behind,

226
00:07:40,630 --> 00:07:43,229
and across the bridge at the junction,

227
00:07:43,229 --> 00:07:45,374
which is empty except for frost-cracked

228
00:07:45,374 --> 00:07:48,084
tarmac and one girl with a handcart,

229
00:07:48,084 --> 00:07:51,276
who drops it and runs ahead to tell everybody.

230
00:07:51,276 --> 00:07:52,294
All nineteen of them come

231
00:07:52,294 --> 00:07:54,101
out of the tall, narrow house.

232
00:07:54,101 --> 00:07:55,955
Ansel shakes your hand with the

233
00:07:55,955 --> 00:07:58,568
yellow mitten and then the green one,

234
00:07:58,568 --> 00:08:00,657
as if one hand is not enough.

235
00:08:01,318 --> 00:08:02,707
"Sea," he says.

236
00:08:02,707 --> 00:08:03,599
"I heard."

237
00:08:03,599 --> 00:08:06,063
They fill the tank with

238
00:08:06,063 --> 00:08:07,557
the last of their diesel.

239
00:08:07,557 --> 00:08:08,189
Every can.

240
00:08:08,189 --> 00:08:09,754
The girl holds the funnel.

241
00:08:09,754 --> 00:08:12,439
When you try to say they should keep some,

242
00:08:12,439 --> 00:08:14,252
Ansel just shakes his head.

243
00:08:14,252 --> 00:08:15,482
"Everything we have,

244
00:08:15,482 --> 00:08:18,051
we have because of a bus," he says.

245
00:08:18,051 --> 00:08:19,717
"Feels right to

246
00:08:19,717 --> 00:08:21,737
give it back to one."

247
00:08:21,737 --> 00:08:23,868
On day 341, he asks the four

248
00:08:23,868 --> 00:08:25,402
of you up to the top floor,

249
00:08:25,402 --> 00:08:28,051
and takes the lamp out of the window.

250
00:08:28,051 --> 00:08:30,463
The lamp that burned every night for Vera,

251
00:08:30,463 --> 00:08:32,367
back when she was on the roof of

252
00:08:32,367 --> 00:08:34,838
this same coach, alone on a motorway.

253
00:08:35,211 --> 00:08:36,601
He gives it to her.

254
00:08:36,601 --> 00:08:38,848
"So somebody knows," he says,

255
00:08:39,367 --> 00:08:41,187
"that somebody has seen you."

256
00:08:41,187 --> 00:08:43,794
Vera holds it for a long time.

257
00:08:43,794 --> 00:08:45,460
She doesn't say thank you.

258
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:46,756
She doesn't need to.

259
00:08:46,756 --> 00:08:48,474
Ansel can see her face.

260
00:08:48,474 --> 00:08:50,279
"Come with us," says Nora,

261
00:08:50,279 --> 00:08:51,793
on the radio, that night.

262
00:08:51,793 --> 00:08:52,470
"All of you.

263
00:08:52,470 --> 00:08:54,178
There's room on the bus."

264
00:08:54,794 --> 00:08:56,076
"Somebody has to keep

265
00:08:56,076 --> 00:08:57,841
the bridge," says Ansel.

266
00:08:58,167 --> 00:08:59,253
"And somebody has to

267
00:08:59,253 --> 00:09:00,748
answer when you call."

268
00:09:01,845 --> 00:09:06,584
From day 343 to day 347,

269
00:09:06,584 --> 00:09:07,911
the coach gets ready.

270
00:09:07,911 --> 00:09:10,750
It sits on the hill road below the long room,

271
00:09:10,750 --> 00:09:11,959
beige and patient,

272
00:09:11,959 --> 00:09:15,221
with the sun drying the last of the mud off its sides.

273
00:09:15,221 --> 00:09:16,708
The depot's name is still

274
00:09:16,708 --> 00:09:18,208
painted down one of them.

275
00:09:18,208 --> 00:09:20,181
Bram lives under it, mostly.

276
00:09:20,538 --> 00:09:22,295
Wes lives in the driver's seat,

277
00:09:22,295 --> 00:09:22,734
mostly,

278
00:09:22,734 --> 00:09:26,424
pretending to drive, until Vera tells him to get out.

279
00:09:26,424 --> 00:09:28,642
The four sections of barrier go

280
00:09:28,642 --> 00:09:29,513
up on the roof,

281
00:09:29,513 --> 00:09:32,796
strapped down tight, in case anything needs a gate.

282
00:09:32,796 --> 00:09:33,908
Wes's list grows on

283
00:09:33,908 --> 00:09:35,709
the wall of the long room.

284
00:09:36,082 --> 00:09:37,345
Things we'd take.

285
00:09:37,345 --> 00:09:38,467
The orange bag.

286
00:09:38,467 --> 00:09:40,296
The turquoise bag.

287
00:09:40,296 --> 00:09:41,406
The flashlight.

288
00:09:41,406 --> 00:09:44,001
The clock, which is still hanging in

289
00:09:44,001 --> 00:09:46,482
the gravel pit, so Vera crosses it out.

290
00:09:46,857 --> 00:09:47,532
The lamp.

291
00:09:47,532 --> 00:09:50,031
The cardboard with the totals on it.

292
00:09:50,031 --> 00:09:51,142
Jump leads.

293
00:09:51,501 --> 00:09:52,050
Tape.

294
00:09:52,050 --> 00:09:53,057
The radio.

295
00:09:53,057 --> 00:09:54,822
Bram's oil can.

296
00:09:55,373 --> 00:09:57,167
Kit adds "my helmet," although

297
00:09:57,167 --> 00:09:59,357
he has never once taken it off.

298
00:09:59,357 --> 00:10:01,506
Nora adds "the sign."

299
00:10:02,042 --> 00:10:04,694
"We can't take the sign," says Wes.

300
00:10:04,694 --> 00:10:06,078
"I know," says Nora.

301
00:10:06,078 --> 00:10:08,440
"That's why I'm going to change it."

302
00:10:09,022 --> 00:10:09,790
That evening,

303
00:10:09,790 --> 00:10:12,040
you find her sitting on the coach steps,

304
00:10:12,040 --> 00:10:14,763
with the postcard in her hands.

305
00:10:14,763 --> 00:10:15,515
Blue sea.

306
00:10:15,515 --> 00:10:16,492
Yellow sand.

307
00:10:16,492 --> 00:10:19,343
The tiny white lighthouse in the corner.

308
00:10:19,343 --> 00:10:21,683
"You said when the roads are

309
00:10:21,683 --> 00:10:24,327
clear," she says, without looking up.

310
00:10:24,327 --> 00:10:25,214
"I know."

311
00:10:25,844 --> 00:10:27,705
"They're not clear."

312
00:10:27,705 --> 00:10:28,489
"I know."

313
00:10:28,489 --> 00:10:30,905
She turns the postcard over.

314
00:10:30,905 --> 00:10:31,497
On the back,

315
00:10:31,497 --> 00:10:33,423
there is nothing written at all.

316
00:10:33,860 --> 00:10:34,589
She bought it,

317
00:10:34,589 --> 00:10:36,660
and never sent it, and never went.

318
00:10:37,321 --> 00:10:39,076
"I'm going anyway," she says.

319
00:10:39,076 --> 00:10:41,186
"I wanted you to hear me say it."

320
00:10:41,186 --> 00:10:43,644
You sit down next to her on the step.

321
00:10:43,644 --> 00:10:45,560
"I'm driving the truck," you say.

322
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:47,909
"Somebody has to stay behind the bus."

323
00:10:48,859 --> 00:10:51,729
On day 348, Nora paints the sign.

324
00:10:51,729 --> 00:10:53,282
She does it at dawn,

325
00:10:53,282 --> 00:10:54,944
when the dead are slowest.

326
00:10:54,944 --> 00:10:57,478
Bram carries the ladder down the hill

327
00:10:57,478 --> 00:10:59,641
and props it against the wall under the sign,

328
00:10:59,641 --> 00:11:01,809
out in the street, outside the gate.

329
00:11:01,809 --> 00:11:04,191
On the other side of that gate,

330
00:11:04,191 --> 00:11:05,926
the yard is packed wall to wall.

331
00:11:05,926 --> 00:11:06,926
Thousands of them,

332
00:11:06,926 --> 00:11:10,779
quiet, facing the canopy, waiting for one more announcement.

333
00:11:10,779 --> 00:11:12,473
Nora climbs the ladder with the

334
00:11:12,473 --> 00:11:14,630
little pot of white paint and the brush.

335
00:11:15,181 --> 00:11:16,730
You hold the bottom.

336
00:11:16,730 --> 00:11:18,353
Wes holds his breath.

337
00:11:18,353 --> 00:11:19,921
Every rung creaks.

338
00:11:19,921 --> 00:11:23,533
Every creak turns a few heads in the yard.

339
00:11:23,533 --> 00:11:24,393
Not many.

340
00:11:24,393 --> 00:11:25,511
Just enough.

341
00:11:25,511 --> 00:11:27,874
At the top, Nora stops.

342
00:11:28,344 --> 00:11:29,676
And for a long moment,

343
00:11:29,676 --> 00:11:31,704
she doesn't paint anything at all.

344
00:11:31,704 --> 00:11:34,117
She just looks at the sign.

345
00:11:34,117 --> 00:11:36,463
At TWO ALIVE, crossed out.

346
00:11:36,463 --> 00:11:38,939
THREE ALIVE, crossed out.

347
00:11:39,473 --> 00:11:42,468
FOUR ALIVE, in her own white paint.

348
00:11:42,857 --> 00:11:43,845
And the frequency,

349
00:11:43,845 --> 00:11:47,006
the one that carried her voice all the way to the sea.

350
00:11:47,331 --> 00:11:49,001
Then she crosses out FOUR.

351
00:11:49,001 --> 00:11:50,115
And underneath,

352
00:11:50,115 --> 00:11:54,411
in big white letters, she paints: SEVEN ALIVE.

353
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:55,886
GONE TO THE SEA.

354
00:11:55,886 --> 00:11:57,227
When she comes down,

355
00:11:57,227 --> 00:12:00,511
there is somebody standing just inside the gate.

356
00:12:00,511 --> 00:12:01,932
The Doorman.

357
00:12:01,932 --> 00:12:03,590
Both hands flat on the bars,

358
00:12:03,590 --> 00:12:06,399
from the inside, for the first time in his life.

359
00:12:06,399 --> 00:12:09,063
Looking up at the sound of the ladder,

360
00:12:09,063 --> 00:12:10,087
the way he has

361
00:12:10,087 --> 00:12:12,409
looked at that gate since day 67.

362
00:12:12,409 --> 00:12:15,139
Nora stands in front of him for a moment,

363
00:12:15,139 --> 00:12:16,456
with the bars between them.

364
00:12:16,456 --> 00:12:17,860
She doesn't flinch.

365
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:19,441
She doesn't step back.

366
00:12:19,441 --> 00:12:22,435
"Look after it," she tells him quietly.

367
00:12:22,435 --> 00:12:24,565
Then she picks up the paint pot,

368
00:12:24,565 --> 00:12:27,470
and walks back up the hill without looking back.

369
00:12:27,891 --> 00:12:32,390
From day 349 to day 354,

370
00:12:32,698 --> 00:12:34,137
the radio goes quiet.

371
00:12:34,137 --> 00:12:37,002
Every night, Ruth calls the Point.

372
00:12:37,002 --> 00:12:38,801
Every night, nothing.

373
00:12:38,801 --> 00:12:40,534
Then, on day 351,

374
00:12:40,534 --> 00:12:43,290
very late, a voice in the hiss.

375
00:12:43,290 --> 00:12:44,304
"Ruth.

376
00:12:44,304 --> 00:12:45,042
It held.

377
00:12:45,042 --> 00:12:46,682
Last night it held.

378
00:12:46,682 --> 00:12:47,638
Barely."

379
00:12:48,252 --> 00:12:48,958
A crackle.

380
00:12:48,958 --> 00:12:50,499
"There are more of them.

381
00:12:50,499 --> 00:12:52,731
They come down the beach at

382
00:12:52,731 --> 00:12:53,408
every low tide,

383
00:12:53,408 --> 00:12:55,823
and they don't all go back when the sea comes in.

384
00:12:56,357 --> 00:12:57,829
Some of them just stand in

385
00:12:57,829 --> 00:12:59,574
the water on the road and wait."

386
00:12:59,574 --> 00:13:01,556
"We're coming," says Ruth.

387
00:13:01,556 --> 00:13:02,994
"We've got a bus."

388
00:13:02,994 --> 00:13:05,028
A pause, full of static.

389
00:13:05,028 --> 00:13:07,672
"Course you have," says Tam,

390
00:13:07,672 --> 00:13:10,283
and for a second, he sounds like he's laughing.

391
00:13:10,283 --> 00:13:11,472
Then he's gone.

392
00:13:11,911 --> 00:13:13,834
After that, nothing.

393
00:13:13,834 --> 00:13:15,599
Not on day 352.

394
00:13:15,941 --> 00:13:17,525
Not on day 353.

395
00:13:17,525 --> 00:13:19,806
Kit stops sleeping.

396
00:13:20,276 --> 00:13:23,139
On day 354, Nora sits down beside

397
00:13:23,139 --> 00:13:24,829
him at the radio,

398
00:13:24,829 --> 00:13:26,631
and says Still here into it,

399
00:13:26,631 --> 00:13:31,339
on the Point's frequency, slowly, so it will arrive safely.

400
00:13:31,339 --> 00:13:33,090
Nobody answers.

401
00:13:33,090 --> 00:13:36,432
But Kit falls asleep against her shoulder.

402
00:13:36,432 --> 00:13:39,711
From day 355 to day 357, you say goodbye.

403
00:13:39,711 --> 00:13:42,341
The hill people give you potatoes,

404
00:13:42,341 --> 00:13:42,806
apples,

405
00:13:42,806 --> 00:13:45,970
blankets, and the boy with the blue bandage.

406
00:13:45,970 --> 00:13:47,063
Only for the morning.

407
00:13:47,063 --> 00:13:49,504
He spends it on the coach steps with Kit,

408
00:13:49,504 --> 00:13:50,757
and when it's time to go,

409
00:13:50,757 --> 00:13:53,872
the two of them do their secret knock on the coach door,

410
00:13:53,872 --> 00:13:55,711
the one nobody else is allowed to know,

411
00:13:55,711 --> 00:13:57,563
and then the hill boy takes off his

412
00:13:57,563 --> 00:13:59,622
blue bandage and gives it to Kit,

413
00:13:59,622 --> 00:14:02,439
who ties it around the strap of his white helmet.

414
00:14:02,439 --> 00:14:04,997
Ansel comes on the radio every sunset.

415
00:14:04,997 --> 00:14:06,118
"Bridge is ours.

416
00:14:06,118 --> 00:14:07,875
Come home if it's bad."

417
00:14:07,875 --> 00:14:10,282
And every sunset, the answer.

418
00:14:10,866 --> 00:14:13,844
On day 357, you stand at the

419
00:14:13,844 --> 00:14:16,396
big window with Nora for the last time,

420
00:14:16,396 --> 00:14:18,265
looking down at the depot.

421
00:14:18,576 --> 00:14:20,426
The yard, full.

422
00:14:20,426 --> 00:14:21,813
The canopy.

423
00:14:21,813 --> 00:14:22,970
The sign.

424
00:14:22,970 --> 00:14:23,932
SEVEN ALIVE.

425
00:14:23,932 --> 00:14:25,239
GONE TO THE SEA.

426
00:14:25,239 --> 00:14:27,020
And the frequency,

427
00:14:27,020 --> 00:14:29,467
so anyone who reads it knows where to call.

428
00:14:29,467 --> 00:14:32,530
"It's a door you can't close," you say.

429
00:14:32,530 --> 00:14:33,200
"A sign."

430
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:35,161
"I know," says Nora.

431
00:14:35,161 --> 00:14:36,452
"That's why I

432
00:14:36,452 --> 00:14:39,033
didn't paint the address."

433
00:14:39,033 --> 00:14:41,963
On day 358, the coach leaves the hill.

434
00:14:41,963 --> 00:14:42,946
At first light.

435
00:14:42,946 --> 00:14:44,472
Vera drives the coach,

436
00:14:44,472 --> 00:14:48,555
with Wes beside her, Ruth and Kit behind, and Bram at the

437
00:14:48,555 --> 00:14:50,829
back with the jump leads across his knees.

438
00:14:51,266 --> 00:14:52,940
You drive the truck behind them,

439
00:14:52,940 --> 00:14:55,815
with Nora beside you, the turquoise bag on her

440
00:14:55,815 --> 00:14:57,988
knees and the orange bag between your feet.

441
00:14:57,988 --> 00:15:00,144
You go down the hill road slowly,

442
00:15:00,144 --> 00:15:01,403
past the depot.

443
00:15:01,403 --> 00:15:03,504
Nobody uses a horn.

444
00:15:03,504 --> 00:15:04,753
Nobody speaks.

445
00:15:04,753 --> 00:15:07,005
In the yard, a few heads turn

446
00:15:07,005 --> 00:15:09,324
toward the engines, and then turn back.

447
00:15:09,324 --> 00:15:10,538
At the gate,

448
00:15:10,538 --> 00:15:12,740
the Doorman stands with his hands on the bars,

449
00:15:12,740 --> 00:15:14,991
from the inside, and watches you go.

450
00:15:14,991 --> 00:15:17,088
Nora flashes the flashlight at the hill,

451
00:15:17,088 --> 00:15:20,011
where the hill people stand in a row.

452
00:15:20,011 --> 00:15:20,497
Once.

453
00:15:20,497 --> 00:15:21,365
Twice.

454
00:15:21,738 --> 00:15:22,540
Still here.

455
00:15:22,540 --> 00:15:25,263
And then the depot is behind you,

456
00:15:25,263 --> 00:15:26,873
getting smaller in the mirror,

457
00:15:26,873 --> 00:15:28,161
until it's just a roof,

458
00:15:28,161 --> 00:15:31,713
and a canopy, and a sign you can't read anymore.

459
00:15:31,713 --> 00:15:34,832
You have lived in that place for

460
00:15:34,832 --> 00:15:35,647
three hundred and fifty-eight days.

461
00:15:35,647 --> 00:15:37,720
You don't look back.

462
00:15:37,720 --> 00:15:39,786
Nora does it for both of you.

463
00:15:39,786 --> 00:15:42,423
On day 359, you follow the pencil line.

464
00:15:42,423 --> 00:15:44,885
Ruth's thin line from the edge of

465
00:15:44,885 --> 00:15:46,657
the city to the circle by the sea.

466
00:15:46,657 --> 00:15:47,564
Back roads.

467
00:15:47,922 --> 00:15:49,034
Empty villages.

468
00:15:49,034 --> 00:15:51,327
A sky that gets bigger and paler

469
00:15:51,327 --> 00:15:52,563
as you drive,

470
00:15:52,563 --> 00:15:55,091
until the trees start to lean all the same way,

471
00:15:55,091 --> 00:15:58,080
as if somebody is always blowing on them.

472
00:15:58,080 --> 00:16:00,279
"Wind off the sea," says Ruth,

473
00:16:00,279 --> 00:16:02,017
on the radio from the coach.

474
00:16:02,017 --> 00:16:03,096
"We're close."

475
00:16:03,679 --> 00:16:05,994
You see a few of the dead in the fields.

476
00:16:05,994 --> 00:16:08,246
Small groups, never more than a dozen,

477
00:16:08,246 --> 00:16:09,377
standing in hedges,

478
00:16:09,377 --> 00:16:12,374
turning slowly to watch the coach go past.

479
00:16:12,374 --> 00:16:13,925
Vera never slows down.

480
00:16:13,925 --> 00:16:15,912
Nobody follows for long.

481
00:16:16,447 --> 00:16:18,642
In one village, the road runs between

482
00:16:18,642 --> 00:16:20,699
two rows of little houses with their

483
00:16:20,699 --> 00:16:21,848
curtains still drawn,

484
00:16:21,848 --> 00:16:25,077
and a single figure stands in the middle of the street,

485
00:16:25,077 --> 00:16:27,056
in a postman's cap, holding nothing.

486
00:16:27,430 --> 00:16:29,468
He turns to watch the coach come.

487
00:16:29,468 --> 00:16:32,134
Vera goes round him, two wheels on the pavement,

488
00:16:32,134 --> 00:16:33,214
without touching him,

489
00:16:33,214 --> 00:16:36,459
the way you would walk around somebody in a corridor.

490
00:16:36,459 --> 00:16:37,255
In the mirror,

491
00:16:37,255 --> 00:16:41,054
you watch him turn, very slowly, to watch you go.

492
00:16:41,054 --> 00:16:43,652
That night, you stop on a hilltop,

493
00:16:43,652 --> 00:16:44,922
the coach and the truck

494
00:16:44,922 --> 00:16:47,075
side by side, the engines off.

495
00:16:47,075 --> 00:16:48,294
You sleep in the cab.

496
00:16:48,294 --> 00:16:50,530
Vera sleeps in the driver's seat,

497
00:16:50,530 --> 00:16:51,203
lying back,

498
00:16:51,203 --> 00:16:54,137
with Ansel's lamp unlit on the dashboard.

499
00:16:54,137 --> 00:16:57,002
And, at midnight, Kit hears the radio.

500
00:16:57,504 --> 00:16:59,928
On day 360, the bridge is broken.

501
00:16:59,928 --> 00:17:01,877
You reach it at noon.

502
00:17:01,877 --> 00:17:03,964
The bridge Ruth wasn't sure about.

503
00:17:03,964 --> 00:17:06,831
A long concrete road over a wide brown river,

504
00:17:06,831 --> 00:17:08,714
where the land flattens out

505
00:17:08,714 --> 00:17:10,846
and the air starts to taste of salt.

506
00:17:10,846 --> 00:17:12,649
It's still standing.

507
00:17:12,649 --> 00:17:14,623
But halfway across, a piece of it has

508
00:17:14,623 --> 00:17:16,591
fallen away into the water,

509
00:17:16,591 --> 00:17:20,256
a long jagged gap down one side, wider than a car.

510
00:17:20,661 --> 00:17:22,238
What's left of the road next to

511
00:17:22,238 --> 00:17:24,523
it is barely wider than the coach.

512
00:17:24,523 --> 00:17:25,950
Vera gets out and looks at it,

513
00:17:25,950 --> 00:17:28,540
with her hands in the ends of her scarf.

514
00:17:28,540 --> 00:17:30,022
"Is there another way?"

515
00:17:30,022 --> 00:17:30,929
you ask.

516
00:17:31,414 --> 00:17:33,213
"Two days round," says Ruth.

517
00:17:33,213 --> 00:17:35,241
Her voice is very flat.

518
00:17:35,822 --> 00:17:37,757
"Through the town upriver."

519
00:17:37,757 --> 00:17:40,783
"We don't have two days," says Kit.

520
00:17:40,783 --> 00:17:42,118
Nobody answers.

521
00:17:42,118 --> 00:17:44,068
Because at midnight,

522
00:17:44,068 --> 00:17:46,607
Kit heard the radio, and woke everybody up,

523
00:17:46,607 --> 00:17:49,694
and you all sat in the dark and listened to Tam's

524
00:17:49,694 --> 00:17:52,319
voice coming in and out of the hiss,

525
00:17:52,319 --> 00:17:54,433
saying the same thing again and again,

526
00:17:54,433 --> 00:17:57,050
as if he wasn't sure anybody was there.

527
00:17:57,050 --> 00:17:58,258
"The gate's gone.

528
00:17:58,258 --> 00:17:59,994
It came down at low tide.

529
00:17:59,994 --> 00:18:02,709
We're all on the rock, at the lighthouse.

530
00:18:03,068 --> 00:18:04,680
Everybody's safe, for now.

531
00:18:04,680 --> 00:18:07,098
But in five days, the spring tide.

532
00:18:07,098 --> 00:18:08,990
The road will be dry for half a day.

533
00:18:08,990 --> 00:18:10,013
And there's nothing

534
00:18:10,013 --> 00:18:11,793
between them and us anymore."

535
00:18:11,793 --> 00:18:14,644
You stand at the edge of the broken bridge,

536
00:18:14,644 --> 00:18:16,056
and look across the river,

537
00:18:16,056 --> 00:18:18,954
toward a line of pale light where the land ends.

538
00:18:18,954 --> 00:18:20,083
The sea.

539
00:18:20,441 --> 00:18:21,413
Three hundred and sixty days.

540
00:18:21,413 --> 00:18:24,436
The same orange bandage.

541
00:18:24,436 --> 00:18:26,240
Nora is holding the flashlight.

542
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:28,344
You are holding the orange bag.

543
00:18:28,669 --> 00:18:30,789
Tomorrow is day 361.

544
00:18:30,789 --> 00:18:33,385
The river is locked in your old yard,

545
00:18:33,385 --> 00:18:34,487
the coach is running,

546
00:18:34,487 --> 00:18:37,585
and the sign over the street says SEVEN ALIVE.

547
00:18:37,585 --> 00:18:39,589
GONE TO THE SEA.

548
00:18:39,589 --> 00:18:41,180
The sea is one day away.

549
00:18:41,180 --> 00:18:42,564
The only road to it

550
00:18:42,564 --> 00:18:44,548
has a hole in the middle.

551
00:18:44,548 --> 00:18:45,515
And in five days,

552
00:18:45,515 --> 00:18:48,233
the lowest tide of the year will open a door

553
00:18:48,233 --> 00:18:50,311
that nobody is holding anymore.

554
00:18:50,735 --> 00:18:52,288
Would you risk the broken bridge,

